The Signal from the Void
The coastal village of Dunmere had always been a 
quiet place, nestled between jagged cliffs and the 
restless gray sea. Its lighthouse, a crumbling relic 
of stone and iron, stood atop the highest bluff, its 
beacon long extinguished. The villagers whispered tales 
of its last keeper, Elias Morse, who vanished fifty years 
ago under a moonless sky. They said he’d been obsessed with 
signals—patterns in the wind, the waves, the stars. Some 
claimed he’d cracked a code that wasn’t meant to be solved. 
Others swore they’d seen lights flicker in the tower on stormy 
nights, as if Elias were still up there, tapping out his final message.
I arrived in Dunmere on a whim, chasing rumors I’d found 
on an obscure cryptography forum. A user named “SilentBeacon” had 
posted a string of characters that caught 
my eye: 
